<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sisodia dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sisodias</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodias</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clan, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guhila-dynasty">Guhila dynasty</a> named after the village of Sisoda, that ruled Mewar from the recovery of Chittor by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammir-singh">Hammir Singh</a> around 1326 until 1949. Its rulers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>, reigned from Chittor and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> and became famous for their long resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty.jpg" alt="Sisodia dynasty" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Udaipur_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodias</a> traced their ancestry to Rahapa, a son of the 12th-century Guhila king Ranasimha, who founded the village of Shisoda, in present-day Rajsamand district, as his capital; his descendants took their name from it. The Rajprashasti genealogy tells it differently: Rana Samar married Prithi, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>, and his grandson Rahapa took the title Rana, his descendants living for a time at Sisoda. The clan claimed descent from the Suryavamsha, the Solar dynasty. The main Guhila line ended with its defeat by the Khalji dynasty at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a> in 1303, and Rana Hammir later recovered the region with the help of Baruji Sauda and his Charan allies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Every later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> of Mewar belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> clan. Its rulers fought the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, Nagor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and helped to weaken the neighbouring sultanates. According to the Sisodia chronicles, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a> in 1303 the men fought to the death in saka while the women committed jauhar, and the same happened when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a> besieged the fort in 1535 and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> took it in 1567.</p><p>Repeated fighting with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> reduced the dynasty's power and lands, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodias</a> finally accepted Mughal suzerainty; some fought in the Mughal army. They were the last Rajput dynasty to ally with the Mughals and, unlike other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans, never intermarried with the imperial family. The Bhonsle clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> and the Rana dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> both claimed descent from the Sisodias.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The poetry, eulogies and paintings the later rulers commissioned stressed their pre-Mughal past and an identity apart from other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> drew on these works for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-and-antiquities-of-rajasthan">Annals and Antiquities of Rajasthan</a> (1829–1832), which spread the view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodias</a> as a superior clan in colonial and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Sub-clans include the Chundawat, Shaktawat, Ranawat and Kanawat, and Sisodia houses ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> such as Shahpura, Dharampur, Dungarpur, Barwani, Pratapgarh and Banswara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sisodia dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mewar, ruled by the Guhila and <b>Sisodia dynasty</b> for over a millennium, was the most prestigious Rajput kingdom, resisting the Delhi Sultanate and the Mughals at Chittor, Khanwa and Haldighati, and its forts, lakes and painting, together with the legend of its resistance, made it central to Indian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rulers descended from a branch of the <b>Sisodia dynasty</b> of Mewar that settled in the Kanthal region; tradition dates their rule to the early 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/partabgarh-state">Partabgarh State</a></li></ul></section>
